A||||1||Philphine||3800sc variations ||||1||
Z||000000||Philphine||10-18-2000||10:00 AM||||<BR>what changes were made to improve output from year to year with 3800sc engines? if i found one of the earlier years (205hp), how hard would it be to get to the stock out put of later years (225 or 265hp)? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||10-18-2000||02:49 PM||||92-93 are 205HP using a M-62 Eaton Blower with a 57mm inlet/TB and 2.1" pulley.<P>94-95 are 225HP using a M-62 Eaton Teflon-coated rotor blower with a 70mm inlet/TB and 2.6" pulley. Yep, they slowed the blower down and go 20HP more. So if I put the 92 pulley on there.....<P>96 and up are 240HP "Series II" using an M-90 Eaton Blower with 70mm inlet and TB. Since the blower is wider, the injectors moved from the intake to heads. 96 is a bastard year in that the trans was the older 4T60EHD rather than the newer 4T65E. You just get a large TC and shift quality control but they are otherwise the same capacity wise.<P>The other differences are varied but not too cosmic from the performance point. The big advantage to the Series II is that the M-90 can be overspun more raising the HP. The disadvanage is that changing the calibrations is far more difficult (flash vs eprom).<P>For the Series I (where I have a fair amount of experience), you can take a Series I engine into the 250+HP range with porting and the 94/95 blower and a 92/93 pulley. But for the best performance, use the matching transmission (4T60E through 96).  A friend just dyno's in at 226HP/301ft-lbs at the wheels with porting, 3" exhaust and a 94/92 blower/pulley combo.<P>None of the blowers last much over 75k miles. The engines are otherwise excellent. <P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 10-18-2000).]||||reg||1
